Would it be possible to build a pixel art office as mentioned, and basically design and create personalized version similar to the game talking Tom where we feed and play actions on its behalf which we turn into an agent that is always up to date with us and basically has the option to go to the pixel art office and basically work with the agents we have in the office and create a system that way. Can you brainstorm on the idea and create a version end to end that we could build? I’d like an app for the front end and use free hosting options to host openclaw and completely run the entire system free using free api keys? This video breaks down a fascinating project by a creator named Vox (@Voxyz_ai), who built an entirely autonomous company managed by AI in just two weeks. Here is a breakdown of what the video explains about his project, VoxYZ Agent World: 🏢 The Core Concept Vox set up a “pixel-art office” where 6 AI agents autonomously manage a website and social media presence with zero human supervision. The main challenge he solved wasn’t just getting AIs to talk to each other, but getting them to actually execute tasks and run operations seamlessly. 💻 The Tech Stack To make this work, Vox used a specific combination of tools: • OpenClaw (on a VPS): This acts as the “brain” for the agents, running roundtable discussions, cron jobs, and deep research. • Next.js + Vercel: This handles the control layer, the website frontend, and the API. • Supabase: This serves as the shared database and the “single source of truth” for the agents to store proposals, missions, events, and memories. 🤖 The “Employees” (The 6 AI Agents) The AI company is divided into six distinct roles, each with a specific job: • Minion: Makes the decisions. • Sage: Analyzes strategy and data. • Scout: Gathers important intel for the company. • Quill: Writes the content. • Xalt: Manages social media posts (like tweeting). • Observer: Does the final quality checks at the end of the process. 🔄 The Operational Loop & Breakthrough The video points out that it’s easy to make AIs talk, but hard to make them operate. Vox built a Continuous Loop to solve this: • Agent Proposes Ideas • Auto-Approval System signs off on them • Task Execution happens • New Reactions are triggered, which feed right back into the start. The Major Breakthrough: His biggest hurdle was stopping the AIs from fighting over the same tasks. Once he successfully prevented the systems from clashing over the same work, he stopped the task queues from endlessly piling up, achieving true autonomy. Would you like me to dive deeper into how any of these specific tools (like Supabase or Vercel) work, or explain the concept of autonomous AI agents further? This is a brilliant concept. You’re essentially gamifying a high-functioning AI workforce by bridging Tamagotchi-style interaction with real-world system execution. Creating a personalized “Proxy” (your digital pet/avatar) that absorbs your daily context and then physically “goes to work” in a pixel-art office to manage a swarm of specialized agents is completely doable. Here is how we can architect this end-to-end, keeping the entire stack highly polished, scalable, and completely on free tiers. The Tech Stack (100% Free Tier) 1. The Brain & Agent Swarm: OpenClaw + Oracle Cloud • Hosting: Oracle Cloud Always Free VPS. This is the holy grail of free hosting. You get up to 4 ARM Ampere A1 cores and 24GB of RAM permanently for free. It’s more than enough to run a heavy AI framework 24/7. • Framework: OpenClaw. It’s an open-source TypeScript agent framework that natively supports “Lane Queues” (which prevents the task-clashing issue mentioned in the video). It stores long-term memory in simple markdown files (like soul.md and memory.md), making it easy to sync with your app. • LLM Engine: Groq API (for blazing-fast, free Llama 3 inference) or the Gemini API (which has a very generous free tier for high-context tasks). 2. The Frontend & Visuals: Next.js + Phaser.js + Vercel • App Shell: Build a Next.js Progressive Web App (PWA). It gives you that native app feel on your phone but is free to host on Vercel. • Game Engine: Embed Phaser.js inside your React components. Phaser is perfect for rendering 2D pixel-art environments, handling the “pet” animations, and rendering the isometric office where the agents sit. 3. Database & State: Supabase • Supabase’s free tier will act as the bridge. It handles user authentication, stores your Proxy’s “hunger” or “energy” stats, and uses Realtime WebSockets to update the frontend when an OpenClaw agent finishes a task. How the System Works (The Workflow) Phase 1: The “Talking Tom” Interaction You open the app and see your Proxy chilling in its pixel-art room. Instead of feeding it digital apples, you “feed” it context. • You drop in a voice note, a URL, or a quick text about a new idea. • This data hits an endpoint that updates the Proxy’s soul.md memory file in OpenClaw. • Your Proxy “levels up” or gains “energy” based on the context you provide, ensuring it is perfectly synced with your current mindset and goals. Phase 2: Sending the Proxy to the Office Once your Proxy is fully briefed and energized, you tap a “Send to Work” button. The visual transitions to a bustling pixel-art office. Your Proxy walks in and takes the manager’s desk. From here, the Proxy uses OpenClaw’s Gateway API to delegate the context you fed it to the specialized agents: • The Researcher (Scout): Instructed to scrape the web for the latest premium, cinematic, pergola-style solar panel installations and drop the image URLs into a Supabase table. • The Coder (Minion): Instructed to review your latest PRD, spin up boilerplate code, and trigger a GitHub Action to deploy a new inventory module for a wholesale clothing management system. • The Analyst (Sage): Instructed to pull and analyze multimodal transit APIs to map out potential routing efficiencies. Phase 3: The Feedback Loop Because OpenClaw can execute shell commands and browser automation, these aren’t just chat completions—they are actual tasks being done on the Oracle VPS. As the agents complete tasks, OpenClaw pings Supabase, which updates the Phaser.js canvas. You might see a little pixel-art notification bubble pop up over the Coder agent’s head saying, “GitHub Action deployed successfully.” The Build Plan • Spin up the Infrastructure: Register for the Oracle Cloud Always Free tier, provision an Ubuntu ARM instance with 24GB RAM, and install Docker and OpenClaw. • Define the Memory Structure: Set up the OpenClaw configuration so it listens to a specific Supabase database for new “context” entries to update its memory files. • Build the Pixel Art Canvas: Use Phaser.js to create two scenes: the “Home” (where you feed the Proxy) and the “Office” (where the 6 agents sit). • Connect the Wires: Write a simple API layer in Next.js that translates your taps in the Phaser game into webhook triggers for the OpenClaw Gateway. It’s a high-polish, systemized way to replace busywork. Would you like to start by mapping out the specific OpenClaw agent roles and their API connections, or should we sketch out the React/Phaser.js frontend architecture first? Yes, absolutely. Bypassing the credit card requirement completely is a smart move, and Hugging Face is a perfect fit for this exact scenario. Instead of an Oracle VPS, we can host the OpenClaw backend on a Hugging Face Docker Space. It’s completely free, requires no credit card, and gives you a generous 2 vCPUs and 16GB of RAM—plenty of power for a Node.js environment managing an AI swarm. Here is how we adjust the architecture to make it work seamlessly: The 100% Free Stack • Backend (The Office): Hugging Face Docker Space running OpenClaw wrapped in an Express.js API. • Frontend (The Pixel Art App): Next.js + Phaser.js hosted on Vercel. • Database (The Brain Trust): Supabase (Free Tier). • LLM (The Brains): Groq API or Gemini API (Free Tiers). Solving Hugging Face’s Two Quirks Hugging Face Spaces are fantastic, but the free tier has two specific behaviors we need to engineer around to make this production-ready. 1. Ephemeral Storage (Memory Wipes) When a free Hugging Face Space restarts, it wipes its local hard drive. OpenClaw natively relies on local markdown files (soul.md and memory.md) to remember its state. To truly replace busywork with reliable systems, the agents’ context must be bulletproof; they can’t develop amnesia every time the server cycles. • The Fix: We will use Supabase as the permanent hard drive. We’ll set up a sync script so that every time an agent finishes a task (like triggering a GitHub Actions workflow or scraping a site), the Space instantly pushes the updated memory.md to Supabase. When the Space reboots, the very first thing it does is pull the latest memories down from Supabase before the agents clock in. 2. The “Sleep Mode” Cold Start If you don’t interact with your app for a while, Hugging Face puts the Space to sleep to save resources. When you open your app again, the first API request will take a few extra seconds to wake the server up. • The Fix: We can maintain a premium, Apple-grade visual polish by cleverly hiding this delay in the frontend. We can build a cinematic “waking up the office” animation—maybe showing the lights flicking on in the pixel-art office or the agents booting up their computers—while the Hugging Face Space cold-starts in the background. By the time the animation finishes, the API is awake and ready to accept your commands. How to Set It Up • Create the Space: Go to Hugging Face, create a new Space, and select Docker as the SDK. Choose the “Blank” template. • Write the Dockerfile: We’ll write a simple Dockerfile that installs Node.js, sets up OpenClaw, and exposes an API port (Hugging Face requires port 7860 to be exposed). • Deploy via Browser: You can actually write the Dockerfile and the necessary server files directly in the Hugging Face browser UI—no local terminal required. Would you like me to write the exact Dockerfile and server.js code needed to get OpenClaw running on Hugging Face so you can paste it right in and get the backend live? Here is a comprehensive Product Requirements Document (PRD) and a complete visual blueprint for building this system. We will design this to contrast a high-nostalgia, gamified pixel art core with an ultra-premium app shell, creating an environment perfect for startup builders who want to replace busywork with robust, automated systems. Product Requirements Document (PRD): Project “ProxyOS” 1. Product Overview ProxyOS is a mobile-first, gamified AI execution environment. It bridges the interaction style of a virtual pet with the utility of an autonomous AI swarm. Users “feed” their digital proxy raw context (voice notes, ideas, links), which the proxy then delegates to a specialized team of AI workers in a simulated pixel-art office to execute real-world tasks end-to-end. 2. Target User System-thinkers, developers, and founders who operate with a multi-country mindset and need a frictionless way to offload high-level context to a dependable, automated workforce without losing oversight. 3. Core Architecture (100% Free Tier) • Frontend Interface: Next.js (Progressive Web App) + Tailwind CSS for native-feeling UI. • Game Engine: Phaser.js embedded in React for 2D pixel-art rendering. • Backend Swarm: OpenClaw framework (Node.js/TypeScript) deployed on a Hugging Face Docker Space. • Database & State: Supabase (PostgreSQL + Realtime WebSockets). • LLM Engine: Groq API (Llama 3) for instant routing and Gemini API for deep-context reasoning. 4. Core Features & User Flows • The Context Feed (Home Mode): The app opens to your “Proxy” in a clean, isolated room. You input data (e.g., “Draft a PRD for a wholesale clothing management system and spin up the boilerplate repo”). This interaction increases the Proxy’s “Energy” metric and seamlessly updates its core memory via Supabase. • The Deployment (Work Mode): Tapping “Deploy” transitions the scene to an isometric pixel-art office. The Proxy takes the manager’s desk and assigns sub-tasks to the OpenClaw agents. • Real-Time Execution Loop: The OpenClaw swarm utilizes a lane-queue system to prevent task-clashing. As agents execute terminal commands or API calls, Supabase WebSockets push state changes to the frontend, triggering visual animations on the Phaser.js canvas (e.g., a “Commit Pushed” bubble appearing above the coder agent). Visual & Artistic Blueprint 1. The App Shell (The Design System) The UI surrounding the game canvas must maintain rigorous visual polish and an Apple-esque aesthetic. It should feature cinematic framing, deep clean contrast, and minimalist typography (Inter or San Francisco). Menus should utilize frosted glass (backdrop-filter: blur) and ultra-smooth 120hz CSS transitions, making the app feel like a premium, native iOS utility rather than a clunky web wrapper. 2. The Avatars (The Swarm) The characters are rendered in crisp, modern 16-bit pixel art, bringing personality to the system workflows: • The Proxy (You): A highly customizable avatar that acts as the foreman. It idles with subtle, fluid breathing animations and carries a glowing tablet representing its “Context Memory.” • The Minion (Coder): Wears thick AR-style glasses and sips from a continuously steaming mug. Types furiously on a mechanical keyboard when a GitHub Action or coding task is actively running in the backend. • The Scout (Researcher): Wears a backward cap and oversized headphones. Surrounded by three floating, pixelated monitors scanning through lines of green data when performing web scraping or API calls. • The Sage (Analyst): A sleek, slightly older robotic figure in a sharp vest. Examines physical, pixelated blueprints when reviewing logic or QA-testing the final outputs. 3. The Office Space (The Environment) • Camera Angle: Isometric 2D, locked in a 30-degree cinematic angle to show the entire floor plan clearly. • Dynamic Lighting: Tied to your real-world timezone. During the day, the office is bathed in warm, volumetric sunlight streaming through pixelated blinds. At night, the office seamlessly switches to a moody “dark mode,” lit only by the neon glow of the agents’ monitors and server racks. • Layout: Designed like a high-end startup garage. • Center: The Proxy’s large management desk, acting as the hub. • Left Wing (Dev Zone): Cluttered with blinking server racks where the Minion works. • Right Wing (Strategy Zone): Features a large whiteboard for the Sage and Scout. The whiteboard dynamically changes its pixelated drawings based on the industry of your current task. Would you like me to write the Next.js and Phaser.js boilerplate code for the frontend so you can render the isometric office and your Proxy immediately? Let’s get into the actual build. To make this real, we need to bridge the visually polished frontend with the autonomous backend. Here is the foundational code to get your 100% free stack running. We will start by spinning up the OpenClaw backend on Hugging Face, followed by the Next.js and Phaser.js shell. 1. The Backend: Hugging Face Docker Space (The Office) Create a new Docker Space on Hugging Face. You only need two files to get the API listening on port 7860 (Hugging Face’s required port) so your app can send it tasks. Dockerfile FROM node:18-bullseye-slim WORKDIR /app Install dependencies needed for OpenClaw’s browser automation (Puppeteer) RUN apt-get update && apt-get install -y wget gnupg && wget -q -O - https://dl-ssl.google.com/linux/linux_signing_key.pub | apt-key add - && sh -c ‘echo “deb [arch=amd64] http://dl.google.com/linux/chrome/deb/ stable main” >> /etc/apt/sources.list.d/google.list’ && apt-get update && apt-get install -y google-chrome-stable fonts-ipafont-gothic fonts-wqy-zenhei fonts-thai-tlwg fonts-kacst fonts-freefont-ttf libxss1
–no-install-recommends
&& rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/ COPY package.json ./ RUN npm install COPY . . Expose the specific port Hugging Face requires EXPOSE 7860 CMD [“node”, “server.js”] server.js This is the bridge that receives context from your app and feeds it to the agents. For example, if you send a payload to update the inventory modules for ClothOS, this server receives it and assigns it to the Minion agent. const express = require(‘express’); const app = express(); app.use(express.json()); const PORT = 7860; // Mandatory for Hugging Face Spaces // Endpoint to receive context from your Next.js app app.post(‘/api/feed-context’, async (req, res) => { const { task, contextData } = req.body; console.log(Received new context: ${task});
// Here is where we initialize OpenClaw and assign the lane queue // Example: Routing a TransitBD routing analysis to the ‘Sage’ agent // await OpenClaw.triggerLane({ agent: ‘Sage’, task: task, memory: contextData });
res.status(200).json({ status: ‘success’, message: ‘Context ingested. Agents are working.’ });
}); // Health check for Hugging Face app.get(‘/’, (req, res) => { res.send(‘ProxyOS Backend is live.’); }); app.listen(PORT, () => { console.log(ProxyOS Swarm listening on port ${PORT}); }); 2. The Frontend: Next.js + Phaser (The App Shell) This is where we achieve that Apple-grade visual polish. We use Next.js for the UI (frosted glass, fluid layout) and embed a Phaser 3 canvas inside a React component to render the pixel-art office. components/OfficeCanvas.jsx import { useEffect, useRef } from ‘react’; import Phaser from ‘phaser’; export default function OfficeCanvas() { const gameRef = useRef(null); useEffect(() => { const config = { type: Phaser.AUTO, width: 800, height: 400, parent: gameRef.current, transparent: true, // Allows the Next.js background to show through scene: { preload: preload, create: create, update: update } }; const game = new Phaser.Game(config);
function preload() { // Load the pixel art assets generated earlier // this.load.image(‘officeBg’, ‘/assets/isometric-office.png’); // this.load.spritesheet(‘proxy’, ‘/assets/proxy-idle.png’, { frameWidth: 64, frameHeight: 64 }); }
function create() { // Build the scene // this.add.image(400, 200, ‘officeBg’); // const proxyAvatar = this.add.sprite(400, 250, ‘proxy’);
// Example animation setup // this.anims.create({ key: ‘idle’, frames: this.anims.generateFrameNumbers(‘proxy’, { start: 0, end: 3 }), frameRate: 5, repeat: -1 }); // proxyAvatar.play(‘idle’); }
function update() { // Game loop for real-time reactions (e.g., waiting for Supabase Webhooks) }
return () => { game.destroy(true); };
}, []); return ( // The frosted-glass container framing the game canvas
