); } The Next Steps With the backend exposing an API on Hugging Face and the Next.js app framing the visual canvas, the next crucial piece is the memory layer. The agents need to know what they did yesterday so they don’t repeat work. Would you like me to map out the Supabase database schema next, showing exactly how to store the agents’ memories and use WebSockets to animate the pixel art in real-time? Here is the exact blueprint for the memory layer. Since Hugging Face Spaces wipe their local storage when they sleep, Supabase acts as the permanent hard drive and the real-time nervous system connecting the agents to your Phaser frontend. 1. The Database Schema (PostgreSQL) We need three core tables to make this system autonomous, persistent, and capable of handling multiple distinct workflows (whether that’s routing analysis for TransitBD or inventory schemas for ClothOS). Run this SQL in your Supabase SQL Editor to spin up the architecture:
– Table 1: Your Input (The Proxy’s Context)
CREATE TABLE proxy_context ( id UUID DEFAULT uuid_generate_v4() PRIMARY KEY, created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT NOW(), raw_input TEXT NOT NULL, – What you told the Proxy project_tag VARCHAR(50), – e.g., ‘ClothOS’, ‘Netso’, ‘TransitBD’ status VARCHAR(20) DEFAULT ‘unassigned’, – ‘unassigned’, ‘delegated’, ‘completed’ energy_gained INT DEFAULT 10 – Gamification metric for the Proxy );
– Table 2: The Swarm’s Work Queue (OpenClaw Lanes)
CREATE TABLE agent_tasks ( id UUID DEFAULT uuid_generate_v4() PRIMARY KEY, created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT NOW(), context_id UUID REFERENCES proxy_context(id), agent_role VARCHAR(50) NOT NULL, – ‘Minion’, ‘Scout’, ‘Sage’ task_description TEXT NOT NULL, status VARCHAR(20) DEFAULT ‘pending’, – ‘pending’, ‘working’, ‘success’, ‘failed’ output_log TEXT – Where the agent drops the final GitHub link, API data, or PRD );
– Table 3: Long-Term Memory (Solving Hugging Face’s Amnesia)
CREATE TABLE agent_memories ( agent_role VARCHAR(50) PRIMARY KEY, memory_markdown TEXT NOT NULL, – The actual soul.md / memory.md contents last_updated TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT NOW() ); 2. The Persistent Memory Sync (Backend) In your Hugging Face server.js, you need to pull from agent_memories right when the server boots up, before OpenClaw accepts any new tasks. This ensures the Scout remembers the premium pergola solar designs it scraped yesterday. // Inside server.js (Hugging Face) const { createClient } = require(‘@supabase/supabase-js’); const fs = require(‘fs’); const supabase = createClient(process.env.SUPABASE_URL, process.env.SUPABASE_SERVICE_KEY); async function syncMemoriesOnBoot() { console.log(“Waking up agents and loading memories…”); const { data, error } = await supabase.from(‘agent_memories’).select(’*’); if (data) { data.forEach(agent => { // Write the memory back to the local file system for OpenClaw to read fs.writeFileSync(./openclaw/agents/${agent.agent_role}/memory.md, agent.memory_markdown); console.log(Loaded memory for ${agent.agent_role}); }); } } // Run this immediately on server start syncMemoriesOnBoot(); 3.  The Real-Time Animation Bridge (Frontend) This is where the magic happens. We use Supabase's Realtime WebSockets inside your Next.js app to listen for updates in the agent_tasks table. When the backend Coder finishes a task, the database updates, and the Next.js app instantly tells the Phaser game to trigger a visual reaction. Update your OfficeCanvas.jsx component to include this bridge: import { useEffect, useRef } from 'react'; import { createClient } from '@supabase/supabase-js'; // Initialize Supabase client const supabase = createClient( process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL, process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY ); export default function OfficeCanvas() { const gameRef = useRef(null); const phaserGame = useRef(null); // Keep a reference to the game instance useEffect(() => { // ... [Previous Phaser config and initialization] ... phaserGame.current = new Phaser.Game(config); // Set up Supabase Realtime Listener const channel = supabase   .channel('schema-db-changes')   .on(     'postgres_changes',     {       event: 'UPDATE',       schema: 'public',       table: 'agent_tasks',       filter: 'status=eq.success', // Only listen for completed tasks     },     (payload) => {       const finishedAgent = payload.new.agent_role;       console.log(`${finishedAgent} finished their work!`);
  // Emit a custom event to the Phaser scene
  const activeScene = phaserGame.current.scene.scenes[0];
  activeScene.events.emit('taskCompleted', finishedAgent);
}
) .subscribe();
return () => { supabase.removeChannel(channel); phaserGame.current.destroy(true); };
}, []); // … [Rest of the component rendering the frosted glass UI] } 4. Animating the Pixel Art Inside your Phaser create() function, you set up a listener for that custom event. When the ‘taskCompleted’ event fires, you trigger an animation—like a green checkmark bubbling up over the Minion’s head, or the Proxy gaining an energy particle. // Inside the Phaser create() function: this.events.on(‘taskCompleted’, (agentRole) => { if (agentRole === ‘Minion’) { // Stop the furious typing animation minionSprite.play(‘minion-idle’); // Spawn a success bubble const bubble = this.add.image(minionSprite.x, minionSprite.y - 40, ‘success-bubble’); // Smoothly float it up and fade it out this.tweens.add({ targets: bubble, y: bubble.y - 30, alpha: 0, duration: 1500, ease: ‘Power2’, onComplete: () => bubble.destroy() }); } }); You now have a complete loop: • You feed context into the high-polish UI. • It hits Supabase and triggers the Hugging Face swarm. • The agents execute the code or research in their lane. • They log the result back to Supabase. • The UI visually reacts in real-time. Would you like to draft the exact prompt system (the “System Instructions”) that the OpenClaw agents will use so they maintain that demanding, high-quality, “Apple-esque” standard when generating your outputs? Let’s build out this interface step-by-step. To achieve that rigorous, world-class visual polish, we are going to rely heavily on modern design principles: frosted glass (backdrop-blur), deep contrast, fluid typography, and pixel-perfect padding. We will start with the most critical interaction point of the app: The Command Center (The Context Feed). UI Element 1: The Command Center This is where you “feed” your Proxy. Instead of a basic chat box, this is designed like a premium, floating command palette (similar to macOS Spotlight or the Linear app) that hovers seamlessly over your pixel-art office canvas. Visual Anatomy: • The Shell: A pill-shaped, translucent dark-glass container (bg-black/50 backdrop-blur-2xl) that lets the pixel-art office subtly show through. • The Input: A completely borderless, ghost-style text area for typing out tasks or pasting links. • The Mechanics: A minimalist microphone icon for voice-to-text context dumps, and a sleek, glowing “Deploy” button that activates the swarm. • The Proxy Status: A small, dynamic energy ring on the left side indicating how much context your Proxy has absorbed. Here is the production-ready Next.js and Tailwind CSS code to render this exact component over your Phaser canvas: import { useState } from ‘react’; import { Mic, Send, Activity } from ‘lucide-react’; // Premium minimalist icons export default function CommandCenter() { const [context, setContext] = useState(’’); return ( // Positioned absolutely over the bottom of the Phaser game canvas
{/* The Frosted Glass Container */}
{/* Proxy Energy Indicator */}
<div className="relative flex items-center justify-center w-10 h-10 ml-2 bg-gradient-to-tr from-blue-500/20 to-purple-500/20 rounded-full border border-white/10">
  <Activity className="w-5 h-5 text-blue-400" />
