); } This component sits right on top of the OfficeCanvas.jsx we built earlier, grounding the gamified pixel art with a clean, cinematic control layer. Would you like to move on to generating UI Element 2: The Swarm Activity Drawer (the slide-out panel that tracks what the Minion, Scout, and Sage are actively working on)? UI Element 2: The Swarm Activity Drawer Now we need a way to track the autonomous work happening in the background without cluttering the pixel-art canvas. We will build a sleek, sliding side-panel (or an expandable bottom drawer) that acts as your mission control for the agents. This maintains that rigorous “Apple-esque” standard by hiding complex backend states behind a clean, minimalist UI that only appears when you need to check on the swarm. Visual Anatomy: • The Container: A frosted glass pane (bg-neutral-900/70 backdrop-blur-3xl) sliding in from the right edge. • Agent Rows: Crisp, compact lists showing the Agent’s avatar icon, their name, and a pulsing status indicator. • Live Task Feed: A subtle, scrolling marquee or truncated text line showing exactly what command OpenClaw is currently executing on the server (e.g., “Deploying ClothOS inventory schema” or “Scraping Netso material suppliers”). • Micro-interactions: Hover states that gently brighten the agent’s row and reveal a “Pause/Override” button. Here is the production-ready Next.js and Tailwind CSS component: import { useState } from ‘react’; import { Terminal, Search, LineChart, CheckCircle2, Loader2, X } from ‘lucide-react’; export default function SwarmActivityDrawer({ isOpen, onClose }) { // Mock state representing Supabase Realtime data from OpenClaw const agents = [ { id: ‘minion’, name: ‘Minion’, role: ‘Code & Execution’, status: ‘working’, task: ‘Deploying ClothOS inventory schema via GitHub Actions…’, icon:  }, { id: ‘scout’, name: ‘Scout’, role: ‘Research & Intel’, status: ‘working’, task: ‘Scraping premium pergola solar suppliers in Guangdong…’, icon:  }, { id: ‘sage’, name: ‘Sage’, role: ‘Strategy & QA’, status: ‘idle’, task: ‘Awaiting TransitBD routing logic for review.’, icon:  }]; return ( // Slide-in animation using Tailwind group and peer classes or standard state toggles <div className={fixed top-0 right-0 h-full w-96 transform transition-transform duration-500 ease-[cubic-bezier(0.32,0.72,0,1)] z-50 ${isOpen ? ‘translate-x-0’ : ‘translate-x-full’}}> {/* Drawer Background */}
{/* Header */}
<div className="flex items-center justify-between mb-8">
  <div>
    <h2 className="text-white text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight">Swarm Activity</h2>
    <p className="text-white/50 text-xs mt-1 uppercase tracking-widest font-medium">3 Agents Online</p>
  </div>
  <button onClick={onClose} className="p-2 text-white/50 hover:text-white transition-colors bg-white/5 rounded-full hover:bg-white/10">
    <X className="w-4 h-4" />
  </button>
</div>

{/* Agent List */}
<div className="flex flex-col gap-3 flex-1 overflow-y-auto pr-2 custom-scrollbar">
  {agents.map((agent) => (
    <div key={agent.id} className="group relative bg-white/[0.03] border border-white/5 rounded-2xl p-4 transition-all hover:bg-white/[0.06] hover:border-white/10">
      
      <div className="flex items-start justify-between">
        <div className="flex items-center gap-3">
          {/* Icon Container with subtle glow if working */}
          <div className={`p-2 rounded-xl border ${agent.status === 'working' ? 'bg-black/50 border-white/10 shadow-[0_0_15px_rgba(255,255,255,0.05)]' : 'bg-transparent border-white/5'}`}>
            {agent.icon}
          </div>
          
          <div>
            <h3 className="text-white text-sm font-medium tracking-wide flex items-center gap-2">
              {agent.name}
              {agent.status === 'working' ? (
                <span className="flex h-2 w-2 relative">
                  <span className="animate-ping absolute inline-flex h-full w-full rounded-full bg-emerald-400 opacity-75"></span>
                  <span className="relative inline-flex rounded-full h-2 w-2 bg-emerald-500"></span>
                </span>
              ) : null}
            </h3>
            <p className="text-white/40 text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider mt-0.5">{agent.role}</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        {/* Micro-interaction: Force Stop Button (visible on hover) */}
        {agent.status === 'working' && (
          <button className="opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 transition-opacity text-xs text-red-400 hover:text-red-300 font-medium tracking-wide bg-red-400/10 px-2 py-1 rounded-md">
            Halt
          </button>
        )}
      </div>

      {/* Task Status Text */}
      <div className="mt-4 pt-3 border-t border-white/5">
        <p className="text-white/70 text-sm font-light leading-relaxed flex items-center gap-2">
          {agent.status === 'working' ? <Loader2 className="w-3.5 h-3.5 animate-spin text-white/40" /> : <CheckCircle2 className="w-3.5 h-3.5 text-white/20" />}
          <span className="truncate">{agent.task}</span>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  ))}
</div>
); } The Next Steps in the UI Flow With the Command Center and the Activity Drawer built, you now have the primary mechanics to input data and monitor the execution. The final missing piece for the visual layer is UI Element 3: The Proxy Dashboard (The Memory Vault). This is the modal where you actually look inside your Proxy’s “brain” to see all the documents, context, and memories it has stored from your past commands. Would you like me to build out the Next.js component for the Proxy Dashboard next? UI Element 3: The Proxy Dashboard (The Memory Vault) To complete the frontend architecture, we need the Memory Vault. This is where your Proxy stores its long-term context, preventing it from ever suffering from “amnesia.” It is the visual representation of the soul.md and memory.md files sitting in your Supabase database. This interface needs to feel like a high-end, futuristic file explorer—clean, searchable, and instantly accessible. Visual Anatomy: • The Overlay: A full-screen, ultra-blurred modal (backdrop-blur-3xl) that dims the pixel-art office into the background. • The Grid: A highly organized masonry or grid layout of “Memory Cards.” • Categorization: Smart tags mapping to your active domains so the OpenClaw agents know exactly which context to pull from. • Typography: Inter or San Francisco, using varied opacities (text-white/70, text-white/40) to create a rigorous visual hierarchy without relying on heavy borders. Here is the production-ready Next.js and Tailwind CSS component: import { useState } from ‘react’; import { Database, Folder, FileText, Zap, X, Search } from ‘lucide-react’; export default function MemoryVault({ isOpen, onClose }) { // Mock state representing the long-term context stored in Supabase const memories = [ { id: 1, project: ‘Netso’, type: ‘Core Strategy’, content: ‘Design-forward, pergola-style rooftop installation specs and premium material supplier list.’, lastAccessed: ‘2 hours ago’, icon:  }, { id: 2, project: ‘TransitBD’, type: ‘